\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{Rd}

%\VignetteIndexEntry{Probability Utilities}
%\VignettePackage{numTheory}

\title{Probability Utilities in numTheory}
\author{Siddhesh Akole}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

This vignette demonstrates the probability functions provided by the 
\pkg{numTheory} package: \code{normal_pdf}, \code{normal_cdf}.
These functions allow users to compute probability density and cumulative distribution.

\section{normal_pdf}
The probability density function of the normal distribution gives the height of the 
curve at a point. While the density itself is not a probability, it indicates how 
concentrated values are around that point. Here, \code{x} is the point being evaluated, 
\code{mean} is the center of the distribution, and \code{sd} is the standard deviation, 
which controls the spread of the distribution.

normal_pdf(x,mean,sd)

<<echo=TRUE, results='verbatim'>>=
library(numTheory)

# Standard normal density at x = 0
normal_pdf(0, mean = 0, sd = 1)     

# Density at x = 1.96 (approx. 95% cutoff)
normal_pdf(1.96, mean = 0, sd = 1)
@

\section{normal_cdf}
The cumulative distribution function provides probabilities that a random variable
is less than or equal to a given value. Here, \code{mean} is the center of the 
distribution and \code{sd} is the standard deviation.

normal_cdf(x,mean,sd)

<<echo=TRUE, results='verbatim'>>=
library(numTheory)

# Probability that a standard normal variable <= 0
normal_cdf(0, mean = 0, sd = 1)

# Probability that a standard normal variable <= 1.96
normal_cdf(1.96, mean = 0, sd = 1) 
@

\section{Summary}
These probability utilities provide a simple interface for evaluating densities 
and cumulative probabilities.

\end{document}
